1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,240
Welcome  back.  Before  we  move

2
00:00:02,240 --> 00:00:04,680
forward,  think  about  lesson  one

3
00:00:04,680 --> 00:00:06,080
for  a  moment.

4
00:00:06,080 --> 00:00:07,439
What  do  you  remember  about  the

5
00:00:07,439 --> 00:00:09,719
comfort  zone,  the  challenge  zone,

6
00:00:09,719 --> 00:00:11,679
and  the  pain  zone?

7
00:00:11,679 --> 00:00:14,920
Which  one  is  where  growth  happens?

8
00:00:14,920 --> 00:00:16,600
Hold  your  answer.

9
00:00:16,600 --> 00:00:19,520
Today  we  begin  unit  one  for  real

10
00:00:19,520 --> 00:00:21,000
with  the  question  underneath

11
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:22,400
everything  else  we'll  do  in  this

12
00:00:22,400 --> 00:00:25,880
unit.  Who  am  I?

13
00:00:25,880 --> 00:00:27,679
To  answer  that,  we  need  to  start

14
00:00:27,679 --> 00:00:29,759
with  one  word.

15
00:00:29,759 --> 00:00:31,359
I  identity.

16
00:00:31,359 --> 00:00:32,960
Identity  is  what  makes  something

17
00:00:32,960 --> 00:00:35,375
itself  and  not  something  else.

18
00:01:12,656 --> 00:01:14,575
Now,  turn  the  question  toward

19
00:01:14,575 --> 00:01:16,055
yourself.

20
00:01:16,055 --> 00:01:17,576
Who  are  you?

21
00:01:17,576 --> 00:01:19,896
How  would  you  describe  yourself?

22
00:01:19,896 --> 00:01:21,336
What  makes  you  different  from

23
00:01:21,336 --> 00:01:23,376
other  people  your  age  or  other

24
00:01:23,376 --> 00:01:24,416
people  who  have  similar

25
00:01:24,416 --> 00:01:25,975
backgrounds?

26
00:01:25,975 --> 00:01:27,576
That  question  can  be  harder  than

27
00:01:27,576 --> 00:01:29,056
it  sounds.

28
00:01:29,056 --> 00:01:30,976
Many  of  us  spend  a  lot  of  time

29
00:01:30,976 --> 00:01:32,976
learning  about  other  people  and

30
00:01:32,976 --> 00:01:34,535
very  little  time  learning  how  to

31
00:01:34,535 --> 00:01:36,615
describe  ourselves.

32
00:01:36,615 --> 00:01:38,816
Self-awareness  begins  when  you  can

33
00:01:38,816 --> 00:01:41,615
look  at  your  own  identity  honestly

34
00:01:41,615 --> 00:01:42,736
without  exaggerating  rating  your

35
00:01:42,736 --> 00:01:44,535
strengths  and  without  talking

36
00:01:44,535 --> 00:01:45,707
yourself  down.

37
00:01:45,987 --> 00:01:47,587
The  source  curriculum  makes  a

38
00:01:47,587 --> 00:01:50,466
simple  point.  People  are  unique  in

39
00:01:50,466 --> 00:01:52,546
many  ways  including  things  like

40
00:01:52,546 --> 00:01:54,627
fingerprints,  eye  patterns,  and

41
00:01:54,627 --> 00:01:57,947
DNA.  But  identity  goes  far  beyond

42
00:01:57,947 --> 00:01:59,747
biology.  It  includes  the

43
00:01:59,747 --> 00:02:02,587
experiences,  values,  interests,

44
00:02:02,587 --> 00:02:05,347
abilities,  beliefs,  relationships,

45
00:02:05,347 --> 00:02:07,107
and  environments  that  shape  how

46
00:02:07,107 --> 00:02:09,107
you  see  yourself  and  how  you  move

47
00:02:09,107 --> 00:02:10,507
through  the  world.

48
00:02:10,507 --> 00:02:12,146
A  person  who  understands  their

49
00:02:12,146 --> 00:02:13,867
identity  can  recognize  their

50
00:02:13,867 --> 00:02:16,187
strengths,  work  on  their  weaknesses

51
00:02:16,187 --> 00:02:17,786
and  make  better  choices  about

52
00:02:17,786 --> 00:02:19,290
where  they  can  contribute.

53
00:02:19,290 --> 00:02:21,530
One  important  rule  before  we  go

54
00:02:21,530 --> 00:02:24,010
further,  identity  is  not  a

55
00:02:24,010 --> 00:02:25,250
ranking.

56
00:02:25,250 --> 00:02:27,250
The  color  of  your  skin,  how  you

57
00:02:27,250 --> 00:02:29,809
look,  where  you  were  born,  where

58
00:02:29,809 --> 00:02:31,849
you  live,  or  the  community  you

59
00:02:31,849 --> 00:02:34,290
belong  to  do  not  make  you  better

60
00:02:34,290 --> 00:02:36,649
or  worse  than  someone  else.

61
00:02:36,649 --> 00:02:38,329
Differences  are  real,  but

62
00:02:38,329 --> 00:02:41,409
difference  is  not  superiority.

63
00:02:41,409 --> 00:02:43,170
For  a  community  leader,  this

64
00:02:43,170 --> 00:02:44,370
matters.

65
00:02:44,370 --> 00:02:45,729
You  will  work  with  people  whose

66
00:02:45,729 --> 00:02:48,370
backgrounds,  interests,  opinions,

67
00:02:48,370 --> 00:02:50,250
and  experiences  may  be  very

68
00:02:50,250 --> 00:02:51,889
different  from  yours.

69
00:02:51,889 --> 00:02:54,330
Respect  starts  with  understanding

70
00:02:54,330 --> 00:02:56,415
that  difference  is  normal.

71
00:02:56,415 --> 00:02:59,614
Picture  two  photos.  In  the  first,

72
00:02:59,614 --> 00:03:01,975
a  group  of  very  young  children.

73
00:03:01,975 --> 00:03:05,014
In  the  second,  a  group  of  adults.

74
00:03:05,014 --> 00:03:06,815
Which  group  would  usually  be

75
00:03:06,815 --> 00:03:08,535
easier  for  you  to  describe  as

76
00:03:08,535 --> 00:03:11,214
distinct  individuals?

77
00:03:11,214 --> 00:03:13,295
The  curriculum  uses  this  comparison

78
00:03:13,295 --> 00:03:15,935
to  make  a  point.  As  we  grow,

79
00:03:15,935 --> 00:03:17,895
more  layers  of  identity  become

80
00:03:17,895 --> 00:03:20,055
visible  through  our  experiences,

81
00:03:20,055 --> 00:03:22,735
choices,  roles,  interests,  and

82
00:03:22,735 --> 00:03:24,575
relationships.

83
00:03:24,575 --> 00:03:27,575
Identity  is  not  one  mixed  label.

84
00:03:27,575 --> 00:03:29,455
Some  parts  stay  with  us,  while

85
00:03:29,455 --> 00:03:31,215
other  parts  become  clearer,

86
00:03:31,215 --> 00:03:33,175
stronger,  or  different  as  our

87
00:03:33,175 --> 00:03:34,248
lives  change.

88
00:03:34,248 --> 00:03:36,688
Now,  it's  your  turn.  Use  the

89
00:03:36,688 --> 00:03:38,448
personal  identity  wheel  provided

90
00:03:38,448 --> 00:03:40,328
with  this  lesson  or  draw  a  simple

91
00:03:40,328 --> 00:03:42,328
circle  on  paper.  Write  your  name

92
00:03:42,328 --> 00:03:44,528
in  the  center.  Then,  write  three

93
00:03:44,528 --> 00:03:46,248
words  that  describe  you.

94
00:03:46,248 --> 00:03:47,608
Around  the  wheel,  add  your

95
00:03:47,608 --> 00:03:49,528
favorite  music,  a  skill  you're

96
00:03:49,528 --> 00:03:51,367
proud  of,  your  favorite  movie,

97
00:03:51,367 --> 00:03:54,328
food,  book,  hobby,  color,  and  a

98
00:03:54,328 --> 00:03:56,248
personal  motto  or  saying  you  try

99
00:03:56,248 --> 00:03:57,607
to  live  by.

100
00:03:57,607 --> 00:03:59,128
Pause  the  video  and  take  about

101
00:03:59,128 --> 00:04:02,568
five  honest  minutes.

102
00:04:02,607 --> 00:04:05,048
Don't  write  what  sounds  impressive.

103
00:04:05,048 --> 00:04:06,581
Write  what  is  true.

104
00:04:06,581 --> 00:04:09,061
Come  back  to  your  wheel  and  look

105
00:04:09,061 --> 00:04:10,500
at  it  as  if  you  were  meeting

106
00:04:10,500 --> 00:04:12,980
yourself  for  the  first  time.

107
00:04:12,980 --> 00:04:15,220
Does  one  page  capture  your  whole

108
00:04:15,220 --> 00:04:16,581
identity?

109
00:04:16,581 --> 00:04:17,941
Of  course  not.

110
00:04:17,941 --> 00:04:19,701
That  isn't  the  point.

111
00:04:19,701 --> 00:04:21,021
The  point  is  that  you  just

112
00:04:21,021 --> 00:04:22,821
practiced  something  many  people

113
00:04:22,821 --> 00:04:24,821
rarely  do  on  purpose.

114
00:04:24,821 --> 00:04:26,940
You  looked  directly  at  yourself

115
00:04:26,940 --> 00:04:28,421
and  put  specific  parts  of  your

116
00:04:28,421 --> 00:04:30,940
identity  into  words.

117
00:04:30,940 --> 00:04:32,180
If  you're  taking  act  with  other

118
00:04:32,180 --> 00:04:33,940
people,  compare  your  wheels

119
00:04:33,940 --> 00:04:36,620
afterward.  Notice  what  you  share.

120
00:04:36,620 --> 00:04:38,621
and  what  is  different?

121
00:04:38,621 --> 00:04:40,581
Neither  similarity  nor  difference

122
00:04:40,581 --> 00:04:42,181
is  a  problem.

123
00:04:42,181 --> 00:04:43,781
Both  help  you  understand  people

124
00:04:43,781 --> 00:04:44,456
better.

125
00:04:44,456 --> 00:04:47,536
Now,  think  like  a  community  leader.

126
00:04:47,536 --> 00:04:49,056
If  you  don't  understand  your  own

127
00:04:49,056 --> 00:04:51,895
values,  strengths,  interests,  and

128
00:04:51,895 --> 00:04:53,776
limits,  it  becomes  harder  to

129
00:04:53,776 --> 00:04:55,296
understand  what  role  you  should

130
00:04:55,296 --> 00:04:57,135
take  in  a  team  or  how  you  should

131
00:04:57,135 --> 00:04:58,816
respond  when  someone  else  sees

132
00:04:58,816 --> 00:05:00,575
things  differently.

133
00:05:00,575 --> 00:05:02,575
Knowing  yourself  does  not  mean

134
00:05:02,575 --> 00:05:04,895
becoming  self-focused.  It  gives  you

135
00:05:04,895 --> 00:05:06,495
a  stronger  starting  point  for

136
00:05:06,495 --> 00:05:08,055
serving  others  because  you

137
00:05:08,055 --> 00:05:09,975
understand  what  you  can  offer,

138
00:05:09,975 --> 00:05:11,776
what  you  still  need  to  develop

139
00:05:11,776 --> 00:05:12,975
and  where  another  person's

140
00:05:12,975 --> 00:05:14,495
strengths  make  compliment  your  own

141
00:05:14,495 --> 00:05:14,997
I'm

142
00:05:14,997 --> 00:05:18,156
The  source  curriculum  closes  this

143
00:05:18,156 --> 00:05:20,917
lesson  by  reminding  us  that

144
00:05:20,917 --> 00:05:23,957
developing  ourselves  should  connect

145
00:05:23,957 --> 00:05:25,397
to  benefiting  other  people.  The

146
00:05:25,397 --> 00:05:27,756
prophet,  peace  be  upon  him,  said,

147
00:05:27,756 --> 00:05:30,957
the  most  beloved  people  to  Allah

148
00:05:30,957 --> 00:05:33,397
are  those  who  are  most  beneficial

149
00:05:33,397 --> 00:05:35,757
to  people.

150
00:05:35,757 --> 00:05:38,717
Keep  that  idea  with  you.

151
00:05:38,717 --> 00:05:41,276
Knowing  who  you  are  is  not  the

152
00:05:41,276 --> 00:05:44,397
final  destination

153
00:05:45,036 --> 00:05:46,196
It's

154
00:05:46,196 --> 00:05:48,396
is  preparation  for  what  you  will

155
00:05:48,396 --> 00:05:50,477
do  with  your  abilities,  your

156
00:05:50,477 --> 00:05:54,117
choices  and  your  time.

157
00:05:54,117 --> 00:05:56,997
The  strongest  identity  is  not  the

158
00:05:56,997 --> 00:05:59,597
one  that  looks  impressive.

159
00:05:59,597 --> 00:06:01,837
It  is  the  one  you  understand  well

160
00:06:01,837 --> 00:06:04,830
enough  to  use  responsibly.

161
00:06:04,830 --> 00:06:07,390
Before  we  close,  look  back  at  the

162
00:06:07,390 --> 00:06:08,990
three  words  you  would  have  used

163
00:06:08,990 --> 00:06:10,629
to  describe  yourself  at  the  start

164
00:06:10,629 --> 00:06:11,910
of  this  lesson.

165
00:06:11,910 --> 00:06:13,150
Would  you  keep  the  same  three

166
00:06:13,150 --> 00:06:14,150
now?

167
00:06:14,150 --> 00:06:15,790
Would  you  change  one?

168
00:06:15,790 --> 00:06:16,950
Would  you  add  something  you  would

169
00:06:16,950 --> 00:06:18,710
not  noticed  before?

170
00:06:18,710 --> 00:06:20,830
Keep  your  personal  identity  wheel.

171
00:06:20,830 --> 00:06:22,269
We  will  build  on  it.

172
00:06:22,269 --> 00:06:24,070
Today,  you  moved  from  the  general

173
00:06:24,070 --> 00:06:26,550
question,  who  am  I  to  a  real

174
00:06:26,550 --> 00:06:29,310
first  answer.  My  identity  is  made

175
00:06:29,310 --> 00:06:31,630
of  many  parts,  and  understanding

176
00:06:31,630 --> 00:06:33,670
those  parts  helps  me  understand

177
00:06:33,670 --> 00:06:36,109
what  I  bring  to  other  people.

178
00:06:36,109 --> 00:06:37,870
In  the  next  lesson,  we  go  from

179
00:06:37,870 --> 00:06:39,950
describing  your  identity  to  using

180
00:06:39,950 --> 00:06:42,163
self-awareness  on  purpose.

